Setting Alarms and using Reminder app are one of the most useful features of Android, even though we like to hate that during night. In this post, we are going to see how to create Alarms in Android. We will be using one of in-built alarm app – <strong>AlarmClock</strong>.

In this example, we will open the built-in AlarmClock app and create an alarm using the <em>createAlarm()</em> intent. To start with, we need to call <strong>createAlarm()</strong> method of AlarmClock class. This is a static method, so there is no need to create an object of <em>AlarmClock</em> class. We will pass two parameters for this method: Hour and Minutes. Minutes can be the value between 0 to 59 and Hours can be same as normal hours value. Assuming the app version is Marshmallow or above, we need to explicitly request <em>WRITE_SETTINGS</em> permission to use AlarmClock intent.

public void createAlarm(View v) {
Intent a = new Intent(AlarmClock.ACTION_SET_ALARM);
a.putExtra(AlarmClock.EXTRA_HOUR, 15);
a.putExtra(AlarmClock.EXTRA_MINUTES, 0);
startActivity(a);
}

We will also show a text showing how many alarms are currently active before exiting the application. To find out the number of active alarms, we need to call isAlarmSet method of <em>AlarmManager</em> class. But before that, we need to create an instance of <em>AlarmManager</em> class. It there is no instance available, it will throw an Exception. Thus, to get an instance of <em>AlarmManager</em> class, create a method and then call it from your main code file. To get the number of alarms, create a variable and initialize to 0. Use a for loop to start with 0 time and keep increasing the value until 24 hours elapsed. During each loop, keep a check of alarms set and increase the counter variable. After the loop ends, you will get the number of alarms set.

public AlarmManager manager() {
AlarmManager m = (AlarmManager) getSystemService(Context.ALARM_SERVICE);
return m;
}

